Batched submission drops the per-dispatch floor 17x, from 617 us to 36 us
2026-09-09. Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G), native Windows, mlir-aie ironenv (v1.4.2,
llvm-aie 22.0.0.2026090201) + XRT SDK 2.21.75, power mode Default. These runs took a
hardware context, one at a time.

Command (from the repo root, ironenv, XRT SDK on PATH and pyxrt on PYTHONPATH):
  python kernels/dispatch_floor/measure_runlist.py

WHY. results/aie/dispatch_floor_npu.log measured the per-dispatch cost through IRON's
@iron.jit path at 617.0 us, of which 169.8 us is a hardware bracket and 447.3 us is host-side
overhead "MOSTLY XRT OUTSIDE THE SUBMIT BRACKET, not Python overhead". That floor is the
repo's go/no-go rule -- an op whose CPU time is under it cannot win however good the kernel --
and docs/SILICON.md 3.4 states that EVERY small-op verdict here is conditional on it. The
same log named the fix at its line 132, and docs/DECISIONS.md recorded pyxrt.runlist as bound
but explicitly unmeasured: "Nothing has been run -- this is an API-existence check and the
next measurement to make, not a result." This is that measurement.

WHAT WAS BROKEN FIRST. kernels/dispatch_floor/measure_runlist.py existed, committed as WIP,
and had never produced a log. Run as-is it reported FAILED VERIFICATION on every batch size
and then crashed with an access violation. Two real defects, both fixed here and both worth
recording because either one silently produces a wrong answer rather than an error:

  1. `kernel(...)` in pyxrt creates AND STARTS a run. The script added such runs to a
     runlist, so execute() was handed runs already in flight. Runs must be built with
     `pyxrt.run(kernel)` plus `set_arg` and left unstarted for the runlist to launch. This
     is why every batch failed its output check.
  2. The cache-resolution fallback looked for the instruction stream as `insts.txt` or
     `*.txt` and fell through to `input_with_addresses.mlir`. The file is `insts.bin`.
     It also took the newest `final.xclbin` anywhere in the cache, which after any other
     design is compiled is a different design with a different argument layout.

A third, cosmetic: the pyxrt handles were left to interpreter-exit collection and faulted on
teardown, after all output had flushed. They are now released in dependency order.

CORRECTNESS GATE. Every batch size verifies its output buffers against the input before its
timing is kept, and the table prints that column. A passthrough that silently did nothing
would otherwise look extremely fast.

