WHAT THIS TELLS US:
  raw pyxrt (160 us) is near the HW bracket (170 us),
  so most of IRON's 447 us host cost is Python/caching overhead.
  Batching helps: 36 us amortised vs 160 us single.
  The host round-trip cost amortises over the batch.

The go/no-go threshold from measure_floor.py:
  current: an op must cost >617 us on the CPU to win through IRON
  with D1: an op must cost >36 us on the CPU to win through
           a batched raw-pyxrt path

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
READING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE FLOOR FALLS BY 17x. The per-dispatch cost stack on one no-compute passthrough, 32 KB
round-trip, same design throughout:

    IRON @iron.jit wall        617.0 us   (dispatch_floor_npu.log, published)
    IRON hardware bracket      169.8 us   (same log, published)
    raw pyxrt, single           ~136 us   THIS RUN
    runlist N=64, amortised      ~36 us   THIS RUN

The go/no-go rule that gates every kernel decision in this repo moves from "the op's CPU time
must exceed 617 us" to "must exceed about 36 us", a factor of 17.

THE "HARDWARE" HALF WAS NOT ALL HARDWARE. Raw pyxrt submits the same design in ~136 us, below
the 169.8 us the earlier log called the hardware bracket. Those two brackets are close to
like-for-like -- both wrap the kernel call and its wait on the same design and payload -- so
roughly 34 us of what was attributed to hardware is host-side too. More decisively, the
batched figure of 36 us is a QUARTER of that bracket, which settles the question the earlier
log left open: 169.8 us is not an irreducible hardware cost.

BATCHING IS A THROUGHPUT RESULT, NOT A LATENCY ONE, AND THIS IS THE LIMIT THAT MATTERS.
36 us is what one dispatch costs when 64 of them are in flight together. A single op submitted
on its own still pays ~136 us raw, or 617 us through IRON. So this reopens verdicts for work
that can be batched -- many independent tiles, frames, or graph nodes -- and does nothing for
a latency-critical single call. Note also N=1 through a runlist (147 us) is slightly SLOWER
than a raw single dispatch: the runlist has its own setup cost and only pays from N=2.

THE CURVE FLATTENS. Read the per-run column of the table above from N=1 to N=64: it halves,
then halves again, then crawls. It is asymptotic rather than linear, so pushing the batch past
64 buys little. The residual ~36 us is
still an order of magnitude above the 3.39 us/run the driver probe measured for runlist
queuing alone (results/aie/windows_xrt_driver_bench.log), so most of what remains is the
actual dispatch and the 32 KB DMA round trip, not queuing.

WHAT THIS REOPENS. docs/SILICON.md 3.4 lists what the old floor closed, with each op's
measured CPU time. Against a 36 us batched floor:

    MobileNetV2, whole model         1720 us CPU    now 48x above the floor
    MobileViT stage-2 attention       240 us CPU    now 6.7x above
    attention stage 2                 240 us CPU    now 6.7x above
    GroupNorm at L <= 18816           233 us CPU    now 6.5x above
    attention stage 3                  34 us CPU    still under, marginally
    attention stage 4                  12 us CPU    still under

Four of the six reopen, and they reopen without a single line of kernel code being written.
That does NOT mean they now win -- it means the floor is no longer the reason they lose, and
the kernel quality question becomes the deciding one for the first time. The attention
kernel's own README argued "the op has to be ~20x larger before the kernel quality is what
decides the outcome"; at 36 us that multiple is ~1.4x for stage 2, not 20x.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- The host-load witness above reads PEER, not CLEAR: another session took about a core during
  the captured run. The NPU had no foreign context, and the amortised N=64 figure reproduced
  at 35.9, 36.3 and 36.0 us across three runs, two of them under a CLEAR verdict, so the
  result is not sensitive to it. The witness is printed rather than hidden.
- One design, one payload (4096 int32, 32 KB round-trip), one machine, one power mode. This
  is a no-compute passthrough: a real kernel's own configuration cost lands inside the
  dispatch and pushes its floor above this one. Treat 36 us as a floor, not a constant.
- Nothing here is an IRON result. The batched path is raw pyxrt; IRON's @iron.jit does not
  use runlists at all, and nothing under aie/utils/hostruntime/ references one. Reaching
  36 us from a real design needs that host path written, which this run does not do.
- The comparison figures 617.0 and 169.8 us are quoted from a run of 2026-09-07, not re-run
  here. Only the raw-single and runlist columns are from this sitting.
- Verification checks that the passthrough moved the right bytes. It does not check that the
  batched runs executed in any particular order, or that they were concurrent rather than
  serialised inside the driver.
- The reopened verdicts above are arithmetic against published CPU times, not re-measurements.
  Each still needs its own paired run before anything is claimed for it.
